  "judge_reasoning": "# Evaluation Analysis\n\n## Task Requirements\nThe task asks two things:\n1. Whether a multi-language abstract is needed for Non-English submissions\n2. If needed, identify the separator between the multiple abstracts\n\n## Screenshot Evidence\n\n**Screenshot 1, 3, 4, 5 (archived arXiv help page - http://arxiv.org/help/faq/multilang):**\n- The page \"Submissions in Languages other than English, and Multiple Language Submissions\" explicitly states: *\"We accept submissions in languages other than English, but do require that all submissions have an English version of the abstract.\"*\n- Under \"Preparation of a Multi-lingual Abstract,\" it instructs: *\"Please prepare English and alternate language abstracts so that one follows the other, separated as shown below\"*\n- The example clearly shows the separator format:\n  ```\n  English version of abstract for your important paper.\n  \n  -----\n  \n  Other language version of abstract for your important paper.\n  ```\n- The page notes: *\"Note the blank lines before and after the separator (-----)\"*\n\n**Screenshot 2 (current arXiv policy page):**\n- Confirms arXiv accepts non-English submissions but requires an English version, showing the topic was researched on the official arXiv site.\n\n## Response Verification\nThe result response states:\n1. ✅ \"Yes\" — a multi-language abstract is required (English version of the abstract must be included) — **matches the screenshot**\n2. ✅ The separator is \"a line of dashes '-----' (with blank lines before and after)\" — **matches the screenshot exactly**, which shows the '-----' separator with blank lines before and after\n\nBoth parts of the question are correctly answered and fully supported by the visual evidence from the arXiv help pages.\n\n## Verdict\n**SUCCESS**",
